Q: What is the correct request body in this Spring scenario and how do I debug it? A: <p>Your question covers several different aspects, so I’ll break it into separate parts.</p>
<h3>Body of request:</h3>
<p>This request body is just an example and will depend on your use case. For example, if the database generates the ID for your entity, you do not need to include it in the request.</p>
<pre><code>{
   &quot;id&quot;: 1,
   &quot;inner&quot;: {
      &quot;id&quot;:1,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;something&quot;,
      &quot;twoWords&quot;: &quot;something&quot;
   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Point: As an architectural best practice, it’s better to use separate DTOs for the controller layer. This helps decouple the controller layer from the repository (or persistence) layer.</p>
<h3>Where your Json transform to java Object</h3>
<p>In a Spring Boot application, incoming requests are handled by the <code>web server</code> (for example, Tomcat). The web server parses the HTTP request and forwards it to the <code>DispatcherServlet</code>. The DispatcherServlet then converts the JSON request body into Java objects and routes the request to the appropriate controller.</p>
